memoLast.ts and regexCache.ts exist for plugin code that runs on EVERY received line (standup, colorkit, text-to-speech). Both hand back SHARED objects, so callers must treat the result as read-only.

memoLast keeps exactly ONE remembered argument. Two call sites that alternate with different inputs would thrash a single memo and never hit it, so each needs its own instance (text-to-speech builds one parser for include patterns and one for exclude patterns for that reason). It compares with Object.is, so an equal string built separately still hits.

regexCache compiles each distinct pattern once, case-insensitive by default, and falls back to a literal substring test for text that is not a valid regex (an unbalanced paren must behave like plain text, not throw). Never pass the g or y flag: a cached RegExp carries lastIndex between calls and would silently skip matches. The cache is bounded and cleared wholesale when full, which only costs a recompile.

Mirrors the mobile app's features/scripts/memo-last.ts and its compiledPattern cache; keep the behavior in step. Tests live beside each file.
